(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-antifungals-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Antifungals Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which adverse effects are listed for posaconazole?","choices":["Headache in about 15 percent of patients, with mental confusion and blurred vision","Bone marrow suppression and hepatotoxicity","QT prolongation, fever, diarrhoea, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and thrombocytopenia","Visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The QT effect places it alongside the macrolides and fluoroquinolones as a drug to watch in combination. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"What enzyme do the imidazoles and triazoles inhibit?","choices":["1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase in the cell wall","Thymidylate synthase, blocking DNA synthesis","Fungal cytochrome P450 14-alpha-demethylase, which converts lanosterol to ergosterol","Squalene epoxidase, which acts early in the fungal ergosterol synthesis pathway"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because the target is a cytochrome P450 enzyme, this class interferes with human cytochrome P450 enzymes too, which is the source of its drug interactions. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which organisms cause systemic mycoses of the internal organs?","choices":["Mucor, Rhizopus and Pneumocystis only","Aspergillus, Candida, Cryptococcus, Histoplasma, Zygomycetes","Epidermophyton, Microsporum and Trichophyton","Chromomycosis, Pseudallescheriasis and Sporotrichosis affecting the deeper skin layers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the organisms the systemic antifungals target, which is why amphotericin B's indication list closely mirrors this one. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which antifungal target exists in the fungal cell wall and has no mammalian counterpart?","choices":["Microtubules, which griseofulvin disrupts","1,3-beta-D-glucan, whose synthase the echinocandins inhibit","Ergosterol, which the polyenes bind","Lanosterol demethylase, which the azole agents inhibit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mammalian cells have no cell wall at all, which makes this the cleanest selective target among the antifungals. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which azole enters the brain and treats cryptococcal meningitis?","choices":["Fluconazole","Ketoconazole","Itraconazole","Caspofungin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It also has the best oral absorption of the group and is excreted in the urine, which suits it to candidiasis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which antifungal classes act on the cell membrane, and which acts on the cell wall?","choices":["Membrane — echinocandins; cell wall — polyenes, azoles and allylamines","Membrane — polyenes and echinocandins; cell wall — azoles and allylamines","All four classes act on the cell wall, differing only in which enzyme they inhibit","Membrane — polyenes, azoles and allylamines; cell wall — echinocandins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That single split is the most efficient way to hold the whole class list, since griseofulvin and flucytosine sit outside both. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"How does flucytosine achieve selectivity for fungal cells?","choices":["It inhibits a fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme with no human counterpart","A fungal transporter pumps it in; human cells lack it","Cytosine deaminase converts it; human cells lack that enzyme","It binds ergosterol, which human membranes do not contain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The missing human enzyme is the whole basis of selectivity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"How does griseofulvin reach the site of a dermatophyte infection?","choices":["It is actively transported across the dermis by macrophages","It is applied topically and penetrates the nail plate directly","It is deposited in keratin precursor cells in skin, hair and nails","It is concentrated in sebum and delivered through the sebaceous glands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is why treatment lasts as long as the keratin takes to grow out. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which tinea infection is commonly called ringworm?","choices":["Tinea pedis","Tinea cruris","Tinea unguium","Tinea corporis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Despite the name there is no worm involved; it describes the annular appearance on the body surface. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"With which agents is flucytosine combined, and for what?","choices":["With caspofungin in oesophageal candidiasis, and with terbinafine in onychomycosis","With griseofulvin in tinea capitis, and with nystatin in oral candidiasis","With voriconazole in invasive aspergillosis, and with fluconazole in vaginal candidiasis","With amphotericin in cryptococcal meningitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is used in combination rather than alone, and its own indications are Cryptococcus neoformans and Candida. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which allylamine is available both orally and topically, and which is topical only?","choices":["Terbinafine is oral and topical; naftifine is topical only","Naftifine is oral and topical; terbinafine is topical only","Both are available only topically","Both are available only orally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both inhibit squalene epoxidase and are used for superficial dermatophyte infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which organisms cause the superficial mycoses of hair, nails and mucous membranes?","choices":["Candida, Coccidioides and Blastomyces","Epidermophyton, Microsporum and Trichophyton","Aspergillus, Cryptococcus and Histoplasma","Chromomycosis, Pseudallescheriasis and Sporotrichosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the dermatophytes that griseofulvin and the allylamines treat. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which organisms is flucytosine indicated against?","choices":["Dermatophytes of the nails and scalp","Aspergillus and the Zygomycetes","Helminths including roundworm and whipworm","Cryptococcus neoformans and Candida"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is given in combination rather than alone because resistance develops readily. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which features of fungal cells explain why antibacterial antibiotics do not treat them?","choices":["Prokaryotic: peptidoglycan wall, 70S ribosome","No cell wall at all, so wall agents have no target","They replicate only inside host cells, so extracellular antibiotics never reach them","Eukaryotic: chitin wall, ergosterol membrane, nuclear membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every antifungal class in this lecture exploits one of these differences, which is also why selectivity is harder than with bacteria. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which adverse reactions are listed for the echinocandins?","choices":["Visual disturbance and hepatic dysfunction","Bone marrow suppression and hepatotoxicity as the principal dose-limiting effects","Renal tubule damage requiring saline hydration","Tachycardia, headache, insomnia, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and blood dyscrasias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Note the electrolyte disturbances overlap with amphotericin B's, despite a completely different mechanism. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"What kind of infection are the allylamines used for?","choices":["Cryptococcal meningitis","Helminth infection","Superficial dermatophyte infections","Systemic mycoses of the internal organs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Naftifine is topical and terbinafine is available orally and topically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Why do the azoles interact with so many other drugs?","choices":["They displace other drugs from plasma protein binding","They hit a fungal CYP450 and inhibit human CYP3A4 too","They induce CYP1A2 and 2C9, lowering drug levels","They chelate divalent cations in the gut and block absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ketoconazole, itraconazole and fluconazole are all named as cytochrome P450 3A4 inhibitors. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"What should a patient be told about taking griseofulvin with food?","choices":["It should be taken with an antacid to reduce gastric irritation","Absorption is increased by a high-fat meal, because the drug is very lipid soluble","It must be taken on an empty stomach, since food chelates and inactivates the drug","Food has no effect on absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is effective against numerous dermatophytes but not Candida, acting fungistatically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which polyene is used specifically for oral candidiasis?","choices":["Amphotericin B deoxycholate","Natamycin","Caspofungin","Nystatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, available as oral preparations and a topical powder, with systemic effects limited by poor absorption. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which tinea infection is commonly called jock itch?","choices":["Tinea cruris","Tinea corporis","Tinea pedis","Tinea unguium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, affecting the groin. The azoles are indicated across this whole tinea group. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which organism does griseofulvin fail to cover, and what is the nature of its action?","choices":["It covers all fungi but only when given topically","It is not effective against Candida, and it is fungistatic rather than fungicidal","It is not effective against dermatophytes, and it is fungicidal","It is not effective against Aspergillus, and it is fungicidal against Candida"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is effective against numerous dermatophytes, which is a different target population entirely. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Why are fever and chills expected during an amphotericin B infusion, and how are they managed?","choices":["Fungal lysis releasing endotoxin, fixed by slowing the rate","A disulfiram-like reaction to the vehicle","Interleukin-1 and tumour necrosis factor; pre-medicate","An IgE allergic reaction; stop the drug permanently"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The reaction is a cytokine response rather than an allergy, which is why premedication works. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What is amphotericin B's mechanism of action?","choices":["A fungal enzyme converts it, then it blocks thymidylate synthase","Forms pores in ergosterol membranes, leaking ions","It inhibits lanosterol demethylase, reducing ergosterol synthesis","It inhibits 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase, weakening the cell wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The drug is derived from a soil Streptomycetaceae, and the leak is why electrolyte disturbance is such a prominent toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Two antifungal classes lower ergosterol and one binds it directly. Which is which?","choices":["Azoles and allylamines lower synthesis; polyenes bind ergosterol already in the membrane","Polyenes and azoles lower synthesis; allylamines bind ergosterol already present","Echinocandins and polyenes lower synthesis; azoles bind ergosterol","All three lower synthesis; none binds ergosterol directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The distinction matters because binding existing sterol acts immediately, while blocking synthesis depends on turnover. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Why does voriconazole's intravenous formulation require caution in renal failure?","choices":["Its vehicle, cyclodextrin, accumulates when renal function is impaired","The drug itself is cleared entirely by glomerular filtration and reaches toxic levels","It crystallises in the renal tubule as acyclovir does","It causes direct renal tubule damage requiring saline hydration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the concern is the vehicle rather than the drug itself, which is an unusual reason to avoid a formulation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which are the three polyene agents?","choices":["Ketoconazole, miconazole and clotrimazole","Griseofulvin, terbinafine and naftifine","Amphotericin B, nystatin and natamycin","Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amphotericin B is the systemic agent; nystatin is used topically and orally because it is poorly absorbed. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Itraconazole is useful in which infections?","choices":["Blastomycosis, histoplasmosis and onychomycosis","Cryptococcal meningitis and relapse prophylaxis","Zygomycete infection including Mucor and Rhizopus","Systemic aspergillosis as first-line therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It has an active metabolite and a wider spectrum than the ketoconazole it replaced. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of the benzimidazoles albendazole and mebendazole?","choices":["They interact with parasite DNA to cause strand breakage","Block helminth microtubules and glucose uptake","Release acetylcholine and block cholinesterase","They inhibit ergosterol synthesis in the helminth membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Note this content is taught in the deck but sits outside the syllabus objectives, which name only antibacterials, antivirals and antifungals. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"What distinguishes tolnaftate's spectrum from the other topical antifungals?","choices":["Works on pedis, cruris and corporis, but not unguium","It is effective against tinea unguium of the nails but against none of the other tineas","It covers Candida but no dermatophytes","It covers systemic mycoses when given orally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It works by distorting hyphae and stunting mycelial growth, and is topical only. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Which adverse effects limit flucytosine?","choices":["Bone marrow suppression, hepatotoxicity, gastrointestinal disturbance and rash","Nephrotoxicity and electrolyte wasting requiring hydration with normal saline","Visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients","Tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is available orally only. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 101"}]};
